WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:04.994
Picture the till at a busy café.

2
00:00:04.994 --> 00:00:07.934
You tap your card to pay 12.50 for lunch.

3
00:00:07.934 --> 00:00:12.164
The card reader spins for two seconds, then says: no reply.

4
00:00:12.164 --> 00:00:12.881
Try again?

5
00:00:13.133 --> 00:00:15.188
Did the money leave your account?

6
00:00:15.188 --> 00:00:17.368
Tap again, and you might pay twice.

7
00:00:17.368 --> 00:00:20.108
Walk away, and the café might never be paid.

8
00:00:20.367 --> 00:00:24.064
The reader can't tell you, because it doesn't know either.

9
00:00:24.064 --> 00:00:24.574
Why not?

10
00:00:24.574 --> 00:00:25.976
And what should it do?

11
00:00:26.267 --> 00:00:28.673
Start with a program on one computer.

12
00:00:28.673 --> 00:00:37.779
When your code calls a function, it returns an answer, or it throws an error, or the whole program stops, and nobody is left to be confused.

13
00:00:38.067 --> 00:00:41.131
Code like that teaches us to expect two outcomes.

14
00:00:41.131 --> 00:00:44.757
So it's tempting to read no reply as no: it didn't happen.

15
00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:47.469
Now split the work across two computers.

16
00:00:47.469 --> 00:00:50.864
The card reader runs a small program; call it the shop.

17
00:00:50.864 --> 00:00:55.802
To take the payment, the shop asks the bank that gave out the card to charge it.

18
00:00:55.802 --> 00:00:57.592
That bank is the card issuer.

19
00:00:57.867 --> 00:01:02.446
They talk over a network: cables and boxes that carry messages between them.

20
00:01:02.446 --> 00:01:06.362
Three parts, and each can break while the other two keep running.

21
00:01:06.362 --> 00:01:08.169
That's called partial failure.

22
00:01:08.367 --> 00:01:09.953
You've met this before.

23
00:01:09.953 --> 00:01:13.676
You text a friend: can you book us a table for Friday?

24
00:01:13.676 --> 00:01:15.193
Two minutes, no reply.

25
00:01:15.193 --> 00:01:16.159
What happened?

26
00:01:16.433 --> 00:01:18.275
Maybe the text never arrived.

27
00:01:18.275 --> 00:01:20.561
Maybe it's stuck, and arrives later.

28
00:01:20.561 --> 00:01:22.022
Maybe their phone died.

29
00:01:22.022 --> 00:01:25.896
Maybe they're in the shower, and will see it in five minutes.

30
00:01:25.896 --> 00:01:28.881
Maybe they booked it, and their reply got lost.

31
00:01:28.881 --> 00:01:30.532
Or the reply is just slow.

32
00:01:30.700 --> 00:01:34.731
Six stories, and your phone looks exactly the same in all of them.

33
00:01:34.731 --> 00:01:40.228
Your text is the request, the phone network is the network, and your friend is the issuer.

34
00:01:40.467 --> 00:01:42.505
Do you book the table yourself?

35
00:01:42.505 --> 00:01:42.900
Maybe.

36
00:01:42.900 --> 00:01:46.780
Then you might have two tables, and one awkward phone call.

37
00:01:47.267 --> 00:01:48.914
Let's run it for real.

38
00:01:48.914 --> 00:01:56.475
The shop sends pay-1, a request to charge 12.50, and starts a timer: 2,000 milliseconds, two seconds.

39
00:01:56.733 --> 00:01:58.395
We'll break it six ways.

40
00:01:58.395 --> 00:02:02.963
Before we do: in how many of the six does the card end up charged?

41
00:02:02.963 --> 00:02:04.071
Pause and guess.

42
00:02:07.300 --> 00:02:09.402
World one: the request is lost.

43
00:02:09.402 --> 00:02:12.927
A box in the network had no room, and threw it away.

44
00:02:12.927 --> 00:02:14.893
The issuer never hears of it.

45
00:02:15.100 --> 00:02:20.659
World two: the request waits in a queue, a line of messages waiting their turn.

46
00:02:20.659 --> 00:02:26.148
It reaches the issuer at 5,000 milliseconds, and the card is charged at 5,050.

47
00:02:26.148 --> 00:02:28.752
Three seconds after the shop gave up.

48
00:02:29.033 --> 00:02:33.200
World three: the issuer's program crashed before it charged the card.

49
00:02:33.467 --> 00:02:35.834
World four: the issuer is paused.

50
00:02:35.834 --> 00:02:45.448
Some languages, Java among them, now and then stop a whole program for a moment to clear out memory, and a bad pause can last seconds.

51
00:02:45.448 --> 00:02:50.255
At 5,000 milliseconds it carries on, and charges the card at 5,050.

52
00:02:50.500 --> 00:02:56.885
World five: the card was charged at 51 milliseconds, and the answer was lost on the way back.

53
00:02:57.133 --> 00:03:03.267
World six: the card was charged at 51 milliseconds, and the answer is just slow.

54
00:03:03.267 --> 00:03:07.408
It lands at 5,051, after the shop has already decided.

55
00:03:07.633 --> 00:03:09.431
Count the last column.

56
00:03:09.431 --> 00:03:11.146
Never charged: twice.

57
00:03:11.146 --> 00:03:13.025
Already charged: twice.

58
00:03:13.025 --> 00:03:14.740
Charged later: twice.

59
00:03:14.740 --> 00:03:16.047
Four of the six.

60
00:03:16.047 --> 00:03:17.845
And the shop's column?

61
00:03:17.845 --> 00:03:20.459
The same in every row: no reply.

62
00:03:20.633 --> 00:03:21.652
So wait longer?

63
00:03:21.652 --> 00:03:23.825
Pick ten seconds instead of two.

64
00:03:23.825 --> 00:03:28.239
A queue or a pause can always last longer than whatever you pick.

65
00:03:28.239 --> 00:03:30.004
Waiting doesn't settle it.

66
00:03:30.233 --> 00:03:34.848
So a call to another machine has three outcomes: yes, no, and unknown.

67
00:03:34.848 --> 00:03:38.276
A timeout is just the moment the shop stops waiting.

68
00:03:38.276 --> 00:03:41.176
It marks the start of unknown, nothing more.

69
00:03:41.400 --> 00:03:46.344
And if the reader turns unknown into no, and says payment failed, you tap again.

70
00:03:46.344 --> 00:03:49.435
In four of the six worlds, that charges you twice.

71
00:03:49.667 --> 00:03:51.221
So the rule is short.

72
00:03:51.221 --> 00:03:53.071
Silence is not an answer.

73
00:03:53.071 --> 00:03:54.551
Treat it as unknown.

74
00:03:55.133 --> 00:03:56.761
So what can the shop do?

75
00:03:56.761 --> 00:03:57.982
Ask again, safely.

76
00:03:57.982 --> 00:04:05.103
Before the first attempt, it gives the payment a unique label, and sends the same label with every retry.

77
00:04:05.103 --> 00:04:07.341
That label is an idempotency key.

78
00:04:07.633 --> 00:04:12.063
Idempotent means doing it twice has the same effect as doing it once.

79
00:04:12.063 --> 00:04:18.996
The issuer remembers the labels it has charged, so a repeat gets the first answer back, not a second charge.

80
00:04:19.267 --> 00:04:23.209
Where the card wasn't charged yet, the retry charges it once.

81
00:04:23.209 --> 00:04:26.247
Where it was, the retry gets the stored answer.

82
00:04:26.247 --> 00:04:27.799
Either way, never twice.

83
00:04:28.033 --> 00:04:31.509
It's like texting: please book a table under the name Sam.

84
00:04:31.509 --> 00:04:35.044
Get that text twice, and there's still one table under Sam.

85
00:04:35.500 --> 00:04:36.655
One quick check.

86
00:04:36.655 --> 00:04:43.150
In world five, the answer is lost, and the shop retries with a brand new label every time.

87
00:04:43.150 --> 00:04:44.377
How many charges?

88
00:04:44.377 --> 00:04:45.532
Pause and think.

89
00:04:48.733 --> 00:04:49.008
Two.

90
00:04:49.008 --> 00:04:51.552
A new label looks like a new payment.

91
00:04:51.552 --> 00:04:54.577
Make the key once, before the first attempt.

92
00:04:54.833 --> 00:04:58.627
Silence has six causes, and the shop can't tell them apart.

93
00:04:58.627 --> 00:05:02.228
So treat it as unknown, and ask again with the same key.
